Wow, you found a nice regression. 

The problem is that the chainable inject() never worked if the server was not started.

I think the solution is to change those two lines https://github.com/fastify/fastify/blob/master/fastify.js#L336-L337

so that we call `ready()` inside the http handler, like so:

```js
return lightMyRequest((req, res) => {
  this.ready(function (err) {
    // figure out what to do with err, we should forward it to lightMyRequest somehow
     httpHandler(req, res)
  })
}, opts)
```

```js
return lightMyRequest((req, res) => {
  this.ready(function (err) {
     if (err) {
        res.emit('error', err)
        return
    }
     httpHandler(req, res)
  })
}, opts)
```

@mcollina 
```js
    if (cb) {
      this.ready(err => {
        if (err) cb(err, null)
        else lightMyRequest(httpHandler, opts, cb)
      })
    } else {
      return lightMyRequest((req, res) => {
        this.ready(function (err) {
          if (err) {
            res.emit('error', err)
            return
          }
          httpHandler(req, res)
        })
      }, opts)
    }
```

This fails with `Error: ERR_AVVIO_READY_TIMEOUT: plugin did not start in time`. Am I missing some step that should signal init being completed successfully?

@mcollina Did some experiments, so it only timeouts if this code is invoked prior to calling `inject()`:

```js
  const startPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    fastify.ready((err, res) => {
      if (err) {
        return reject(err)
      }
      resolve()
    })
  })
  startPromise.then(<...>)
```

Obviously I can just remove it, but is the behaviour correct? Shouldn't it work if app was readied previously?

